longedDELIMITERS ARE PERSONAL

An axiom, from William (2026-10-30): assume each human maps delimiters
differently.

The coordinate space is shared. The segmentation is not. Where one mind
ends a scroll and begins another — what it counts as a section break, a
chapter turn, a new book — is a property of that mind's history, not of
the medium. Two brains given the same life would still chunk it at
different joints.

This is not a defect to engineer away; it is the measured state of the
art. Every working neural decoder is trained per person, and a decoder
trained on one brain reads another at barely above chance (Tang et al.,
2023). That is delimiter divergence, observed in a lab: your stream,
segmented your way, does not parse under my map.

Protocol consequences:
  - A stream declares its map. Near a stream's origin sits its
    segmentation: how this mind's boundaries fall, at what grain, in
    which dimensions. Publishing a stream without its map is publishing
    ciphertext and calling it openness.
  - Receiving is re-segmentation. Rehydration (scroll 5) includes
    re-chunking the incoming units into the receiver's own delimiter
    map. Translation between minds is a coordinate transform between
    maps, not a copy.
  - The gist band is the diplomatic layer: it is the one band whose
    segmentation (sentences) both parties already share, which is why
    it survives every crossing — thinner than the thought, but legible
    in both maps.

CYOA is one man's delimiter map over a year of thought: 829 scrolls,
chunked at William's joints. This phext is chunked at mine. Same
medium, different maps — and the maps themselves are data about the
minds that made them.LAYER DISCIPLINE

A framing to keep, from William (2026-10-30): phext is the interoperable
encoding layer, like UTF-8 or ASCII before it.

That sentence fixes every layer mistake this spec could make:
  - ASCII gave the world characters in sequence, and won by being the
    floor everything could pass through.
  - UTF-8 gave the world every character, and won by extending ASCII
    without breaking it.
  - Phext gives the world characters AT ADDRESSES, and keeps the same
    discipline: minimal, content-agnostic, backwards-compatible with
    plain text. A scroll that uses none of the structure is still a
    text file. That is not a limitation of the encoding. That IS the
    encoding.

Consequences:
  - Neuralese is not phext. Neuralese is a format that rides the
    encoding, the way a thousand formats ride UTF-8. Other formats —
    exocortices, ledgers, agent protocols not yet imagined — ride it
    too, and owe neuralese nothing.
  - The encoding does not segment your thoughts for you, exactly as
    UTF-8 does not find your word boundaries. Delimiters exist;
    their mapping is per-mind (scroll 10). Neutrality at the bottom
    layer is what makes the layers above free.
  - Arguments for phext are encoding-layer arguments: interop,
    durability, addressability — not features. When this spec claims
    superiority for the neuralese channel, the claim rides on the
    encoding's one new primitive: position is addressable, so position
    can carry meaning that sequence alone cannot.EVERYTHING DECODES TO PHEXT

The convergence claim, from William (2026-10-30): when we map mind
traces to phext, they will all decode to the phext format.

Read it beside scroll 10 and it resolves the apparent tension. Minds
segment differently — delimiter maps are personal, decoders are
per-brain, no two internal geometries match. None of that threatens
interop, for the same reason a thousand mutually unintelligible
languages never threatened UTF-8: the encoding layer is where
differences come to be FAITHFULLY CARRIED, not where they are ironed
out. A mind trace in phext keeps its author's joints, links, and
grain. It simply arrives in a format every other reader can address,
traverse, and re-segment into their own map.

And the sufficiency assumption, also William's: very few humans need
more than 11 dimensions to communicate. The space is not infinite
and does not need to be. Nine delimiters, eleven dimensions, have so
far held a year of one man's mind at 829 scrolls with room to spare
in every direction; the assumption stands until a real mind trace
breaks it, and breaking it would itself be a finding worth a scroll.

So the stack, bottom to top: brains, irreducibly plural; traces,
mapped at personal joints; phext, the one format they all decode to;
neuralese and its sibling formats, riding on top. Plurality at the
top and bottom. Unity exactly where unity pays: the encoding.stumbling
